e leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{21}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is in row echelon form." "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{21}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 0 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 4\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is in row echelon form."] [:db/cas 17592186341839 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186341839 :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-3$, $4$, and $2$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-3+4+2=3.$" "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-3$, $4$, and $2$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-3+4+2=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186341911 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st and $3$rd rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st and $3$rd rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186341911 :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st and $3$rd rows of $A$ are $4$ and $-1$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $4+(-1)=3.$" "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st and $3$rd rows of $A$ are $4$ and $-1$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $4+(-1)=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186341912 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186341912 :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st and $4$th rows of $A$ are $1$ and $-3$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $1+(-3)=-2.$" "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st and $4$th rows of $A$ are $1$ and $-3$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $1+(-3)=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186341913 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186341913 :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $1$ and $6$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $1+6=7.$" "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $1$ and $6$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $1+6=7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186341914 :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186341914 :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows of $A$ are $-7$, $5$, and $3$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-7+5+3=1.$" "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd rows of $A$ are $-7$, $5$, and $3$, respectively.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $-7+5+3=1.$"] [:db/cas 175921863